s states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(1(3)^{2}-{1}^{2})-(0(1)^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337831 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337832 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337833 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337834 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337835 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1041 "selection" 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1104 #edb/ref 17592186337833 13194139535212 true] [17592186337830 1110 #uuid "d7b3f228-f935-51b9-9073-132a5358027a" 13194139535212 true] [17592186337831 1034 "12" 13194139535212 true] [17592186337831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535212 true] [17592186337831 1111 #uuid "58967ece-31e0-56c0-8017-7d76a93d43ff" 13194139535212 true] [17592186337832 1034 "10" 13194139535212 true] [1759218633